 2027, which is slower than in 2026. These are announced plans, not funded orders. I found no backlog or equipment-price data.\n- **Funding is the problem (n3).** Debt is open but costs more: utility bond spreads widened 30bp to 165bp in Q3. Equity is largely shut. Pellham pulled its C$2.4B raise and has said it will cut 2027 transformer orders by about 40% and defer two substation programmes. Norvane priced a C$1.1B bond comfortably and can probably fund its plan mostly with debt.\n- **VLTX is exposed to exactly that risk.** Utilities are 62% of its orders. Pellham is 35% of those and Norvane 21%. Pellham is the largest buyer, and it is the one cutting orders. Pellham's cuts therefore hit VLTX's near-term orders harder than the headline capex growth suggests.\n- **The options give no reason to buy now (n4).** Implied volatility is 27% against a 2-year median of 26%, and skew is flat. That makes the LEAPS fairly priced, neither cheap nor rich. I had no strikes, expiries, premiums or realized volatility.\n\n**What would change this:** Pellham restoring its financing or reinstating the deferred programmes. Evidence in VLTX's backlog or orders that the capex is turning into real orders would also help. A pullback in implied volatility, or in the share price, would make the entry cheaper.\n\n**Gaps:** Nobody sized Pellham's funding gap, leverage or rating headroom. I also don't know your current position size or how much risk you want to take, which affect whether adding makes sense for you.","ttft_ms":3041,"type":"result","duration_ms":49449,"uuid":"e8bb50c1-0f3b-4903-a812-24b41c948366","ttft_stream_ms":1833,"time_to_request_ms":192,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":12,"system_prompt":11,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":10,"input_attachments":42,"input_hooks":5,"conversation_append":0,"transcript_persist":9,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":18,"init_frame_send":7,"engine_pickup":2,"loop_entry":5,"message_prep":4,"model_config_wait":1,"autocompact":9,"request